<h1>The Embedded Debug Hierarchy</h1>
<p class="tagline">Why readsb has six distinct levels of debug output — and why the order is determined by cost, not preference.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="1">
    <span>1. The Core Problem: You Can't Always Attach a Debugger</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>In desktop software, when something goes wrong you attach <code>gdb</code>, set a breakpoint, and inspect state interactively. In embedded and systems software — and readsb runs on Raspberry Pis, NAS boxes, minimal Linux containers — this is often impossible or impractical:</p>

<ul style="margin:0 0 1rem 1.5rem; line-height:2;">
  <li>The device may have no interactive shell during normal operation</li>
  <li>Attaching a debugger pauses the process — a 35-callbacks-per-second USB read loop will drop data</li>
  <li>The bug may only appear after hours of runtime, not during a debug session</li>
  <li>The hardware may be headless, remote, or both</li>
</ul>

<p>The solution is <strong>built-in instrumentation</strong> — output mechanisms baked into the binary itself, ranging from "always on" to "requires recompile." Each level has a cost/availability tradeoff. The hierarchy exists because not every question is worth the same price to answer.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="2">
    <span>2. The Six Levels — Overview</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>From lowest cost/highest availability at the top, to highest cost/lowest availability at the bottom:</p>

<div class="pyramid">
  <div class="level-row">
    <div class="level-badge l1">Level 1<br>#ifdef compile</div>
    <div class="level-body"><strong>Compile-time guards</strong> — zero runtime overhead; removed from binary entirely. Used when the debug code itself is expensive or changes behaviour.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="level-row">
    <div class="level-badge l2">Level 2<br>always-on stderr</div>
    <div class="level-body"><strong>Unconditional fprintf to stderr</strong> — FATAL errors and one-time startup messages. Low-frequency, always needed, no rate limiting required.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="level-row">
    <div class="level-badge l3">Level 3<br>antiSpam stderr</div>
    <div class="level-body"><strong>Rate-limited stderr</strong> — warnings in tight loops. The antiSpam pattern suppresses repeats; one message per "episode" instead of one per callback.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="level-row">
    <div class="level-badge l4">Level 4<br>--debug flags</div>
    <div class="level-body"><strong>Modes.debug_* runtime flags</strong> — enabled with <code>--debug &lt;chars&gt;</code>. Zero cost when off; verbose when on. No recompile needed.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="level-row">
    <div class="level-badge l5">Level 5<br>commented-out</div>
    <div class="level-body"><strong>Commented-out fprintf</strong> — too spammy even for debug mode. Uncomment and recompile to see every loop iteration. Left in the code as breadcrumbs for the next developer.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="level-row">
    <div class="level-badge l6">Level 6<br>crash on demand</div>
    <div class="level-body"><strong>Intentional crash / segfault</strong> — <code>--devel provokeSegfault</code>. Tests that crash reporting, core dumps, and monitoring infrastructure actually work.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="axes">
  <div class="axis">
    <div class="axis-label">↑ Cost increases downward</div>
    Levels 1→6: more disruptive, requires more from the developer (recompile, restart, data loss)
  </div>
  <div class="axis">
    <div class="axis-label">↑ Availability decreases downward</div>
    Level 2 is always there; Level 5–6 requires you to act before you can observe
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="3">
    <span>3. Levels 1–3 — Always-Available Output</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<h3>Level 1 — Compile-time #ifdef</h3>
<p>The most extreme version: the debug code literally does not exist in the binary unless you define the flag at compile time. Used in <span class="filepath">crc.c</span> for the CRC syndrome table construction — an expensive, verbose process that runs only once at startup but would produce hundreds of lines:</p>

<pre><span class="pp">#ifdef CRCDEBUG</span>
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"Preparing syndrome table to correct up to %d-bit errors "</span>
            <span class="str">"(detecting %d-bit errors) in a %d-bit message (max %d entries)\n"</span>,
            max_correct, max_detect, bits, maxsize);
<span class="pp">#endif</span>

    <span class="cm">/* ... hundreds of lines later ... */</span>

<span class="pp">#ifdef CRCDEBUG</span>
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"Sorting syndromes..\n"</span>);
<span class="pp">#endif</span>
    qsort(table, usedsize, <span class="kw">sizeof</span>(struct errorinfo), syndrome_compare);

<span class="pp">#ifdef CRCDEBUG</span>
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"Discarded %d collisions.\n"</span>, usedsize - j);
<span class="pp">#endif</span></pre>

<p>Also in <span class="filepath">threadpool.c</span>: <code>#undef DEBUG</code> at the top — the developer explicitly <em>removes</em> a debug define before shipping, rather than relying on the define not being set:</p>
<pre><span class="pp">#undef DEBUG</span>   <span class="cm">// intentional: force off even if -DDEBUG is in CFLAGS</span>

<span class="pp">#ifdef DEBUG</span>
<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;stdio.h&gt;</span>
<span class="pp">#endif</span></pre>

<p><strong>Why use level 1?</strong> When the debug output is so verbose it would obscure normal startup messages, when the debug code adds measurable overhead (extra allocations, extra loops), or when it changes observable timing.</p>

<h3>Level 2 — Unconditional stderr: FATAL and startup</h3>
<p>Unconditional prints serve two categories: <strong>fatal errors</strong> (the program cannot continue) and <strong>startup confirmation</strong> (one-time messages that confirm the system initialized correctly). From <span class="filepath">sdr_rtlsdr.c</span>:</p>

<pre><span class="cm">// Fatal — these fire once, then the function returns false</span>
fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"FATAL: rtlsdr: no supported devices found.\n"</span>);

<span class="cm">// Startup confirmation — also fires once, on success</span>
fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"rtlsdr: using device #%d: %s (%s, %s, SN %s)\n"</span>,
        dev_index, rtlsdr_get_device_name(dev_index),
        manufacturer, product, serial);

<span class="cm">// Unusual hardware warning — fires once</span>
fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"\n\nrtlsdr: BAD TUNER, 1090 reception will be TERRIBLE\n\n\n"</span>);</pre>

<p><strong>Why unconditional?</strong> These events happen at most once per process lifetime. There is no spam risk. And users <em>always</em> need to know about fatal errors — gating them behind a flag would hide startup failures from production deployments.</p>

<h3>Level 3 — Rate-limited (antiSpam) stderr</h3>
<p>Some conditions occur constantly in hot paths. Without rate-limiting, one warning per USB callback = 35 identical lines per second flooding the terminal and drowning out everything else. readsb uses two antiSpam patterns:</p>

<p><strong>Pattern A — time-based</strong> (from <span class="filepath">sdr_rtlsdr.c:398</span>):</p>
<pre><span class="kw">static</span> <span class="ty">int64_t</span> antiSpam;            <span class="cm">// stores the "next allowed print" timestamp</span>

<span class="kw">if</span> (len != Modes.sdr_buf_size) {
    <span class="kw">if</span> (mstime() &gt; antiSpam) {      <span class="cm">// only print if enough time has passed</span>
        antiSpam = mstime() + <span class="num">10</span> * SECONDS;
        fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"weirdness: rtlsdr gave us a block with unusual size\n"</span>);
    }
}</pre>

<p><strong>Pattern B — count-based</strong> (from <span class="filepath">sdr_rtlsdr.c:423</span>):</p>
<pre><span class="kw">static int</span> antiSpam;               <span class="cm">// a countdown counter</span>

<span class="kw">if</span> (--antiSpam &lt;= <span class="num">0</span> &amp;&amp; !Modes.exit) {
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"FIFO dropped, suppressing for 30 seconds.\n"</span>);
    antiSpam = <span class="num">300</span>;               <span class="cm">// 300 callbacks ÷ 35/sec ≈ 8.5 seconds</span>
}</pre>

<p><code>api.c</code> even promotes this into a helper function:</p>
<pre><span class="kw">static int</span> <span class="fn">antiSpam</span>(<span class="ty">int64_t</span> *nextPrint, <span class="ty">int64_t</span> interval) {
    <span class="ty">int64_t</span> now = mstime();
    <span class="kw">if</span> (now &gt; *nextPrint) { *nextPrint = now + interval; <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="num">1</span>; }
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="num">0</span>;
}</pre>

<div class="callout tip">
  <strong>Time-based vs count-based:</strong> Time-based (<code>mstime() > antiSpam</code>) gives a predictable wall-clock suppression window and is safe to use across threads (just needs an int64_t per call site). Count-based is simpler for callback-rate code where you know the call frequency. Both are <code>static</code> inside the function — they survive across calls without polluting global state.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="4">
    <span>4. Levels 4–5 — Opt-in Output</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<h3>Level 4 — Modes.debug_* runtime flags</h3>
<p>For subsystem-specific verbose output that is too noisy for normal operation but doesn't require a recompile to enable. The pattern is always <code>if (Modes.debug_X)</code> gating a <code>fprintf</code>:</p>

<pre><span class="cm">// net_io.c — only prints when --debug n is active</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> (Modes.debug_net) {
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"net_io: connection attempt %s:%s\n"</span>, host, port_s);
}

<span class="cm">// sdr_rtlsdr.c — debug AND rate-limited (belt-and-suspenders)</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> (Modes.debug_sampleCounter &amp;&amp; --antiSpam2 &lt;= <span class="num">0</span>) {
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"sampleTimestamp: %020llu\n"</span>, outbuf-&gt;sampleTimestamp);
    antiSpam2 = <span class="num">3000</span>;
}</pre>

<p>Notice: level 4 can be <em>combined</em> with level 3 — even when a debug flag is on, high-frequency code still rate-limits its output.</p>

<p><strong>Why not just always print?</strong> In production, <code>debug_net</code> would flood stderr with every TCP event — potentially thousands of lines per second on a busy aggregator node. The flag lets a developer investigate network issues in production without stopping the process.</p>

<h3>Level 5 — Commented-out fprintf</h3>
<p>The most intrusive instrumentation short of a debugger. These are prints that are too verbose even for the debug flags, requiring a recompile to enable. They are deliberately left in the codebase as navigation aids — "if you're debugging this function, here's where to look."</p>

<pre><span class="cm">// convert.c:52 — inside the lookup table initialization loop</span>
<span class="cm">// Every (I,Q) pair → magnitude value: 65,536 lines if uncommented</span>
<span class="cm">//fprintf(stderr, "%d %d %.2f\n", i, q, magsq);</span>

<span class="cm">// sdr_rtlsdr.c:307 — numgains immediately after driver opens</span>
<span class="cm">//fprintf(stderr, "numgains: %d\n", RTLSDR.numgains);</span>

<span class="cm">// readsb.c:1679 — at the top of parse_opt(), traces every CLI option</span>
<span class="cm">//fprintf(stderr, "parse_opt(%d, %s, argp_state*)\n", key, arg);</span>

<span class="cm">// aircraft.c:31 — fires on every aircraft allocation from free list</span>
<span class="cm">//fprintf(stderr, "alloc from freelist\n");</span></pre>

<div class="callout warn">
  <strong>Breadcrumb discipline:</strong> A commented-out print should be removed <em>only</em> if you're confident no future developer will need it. In a codebase like readsb, where the original authors aren't always available, these comments say "I was here, I debugged this exact point, here's the print that helped me."
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="5">
    <span>5. Level 6 — Crash on Demand + Why This Matters</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>The strangest level: intentional process destruction. <code>--devel provokeSegfault</code> in <span class="filepath">readsb.c:3241</span>:</p>

<pre><span class="kw">if</span> (Modes.debug_provoke_segfault) {
    msleep(<span class="num">666</span>);   <span class="cm">// let the process fully initialize first</span>
    fprintf(stderr, <span class="str">"devel=provokeSegfault -> provoking SEGFAULT now!\n"</span>);
    <span class="kw">int</span> *a = NULL;
    *a = <span class="num">0</span>;         <span class="cm">// guaranteed SIGSEGV</span>
}</pre>

<p><strong>Why would you deliberately crash the process?</strong> Because <em>the crash infrastructure needs testing too</em>. In production deployments:</p>

<ul style="margin:0 0 1rem 1.5rem; line-height:2;">
  <li>Does the systemd unit actually restart after a crash?</li>
  <li>Does the core dump handler write a useful file?</li>
  <li>Does the monitoring alert fire within the expected SLA?</li>
  <li>Does the init script suppress the "started" log line if readsb immediately dies?</li>
</ul>

<p>You cannot answer these questions by just <em>reading</em> the configuration. You have to <em>observe the actual behavior</em>. <code>provokeSegfault</code> lets you fire a real crash on demand, in the real deployment environment, at a moment of your choosing — without waiting for a genuine bug to manifest.</p>

<div class="callout red">
  <strong>The hierarchy is a cost ladder, not a preference order.</strong> You don't "start at level 1 and work down." You ask: <em>how much does it cost to observe this, and what is the minimum cost that gives me the answer?</em><br><br>
  — Unknown bug? Start at level 4 (runtime flags, no restart if already running via a signal or --devel).<br>
  — Tight loop too slow? The print you added is at level 5 — comment it out and move to level 4.<br>
  — System never sends crashes to monitoring? Use level 6 deliberately once to verify.
</div>

<h3>Why is the order cost-driven?</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Can enable without…</th><th>Performance cost when off</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1 — #ifdef</td><td>Requires recompile</td><td>Zero — code doesn't exist</td><td>None; dead code</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2 — always-on</td><td>Nothing — always active</td><td>One write per program lifetime</td><td>None; rare event</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3 — antiSpam</td><td>Nothing — always active</td><td>One compare per call</td><td>None if rate-limited correctly</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4 — debug flags</td><td>Restart required</td><td>One branch per call</td><td>Flooding if flag left on</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5 — commented</td><td>Requires recompile</td><td>Zero — commented out</td><td>Extreme spam if uncommented in loops</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6 — crash</td><td>Restart required</td><td>Zero — never reached normally</td><td>Process dies; use deliberately</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="6">
    <span>6. Often Confused With: External Tools</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Internal instrumentation (this hierarchy)</th><th>External tools (gdb, strace, valgrind)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Availability</td><td>Works on any deployment — headless Pi, container, remote server</td><td>Requires tool binary + sometimes OS features (ptrace)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Timing impact</td><td>Minimal — one branch, one write</td><td>High — strace adds syscall overhead; valgrind slows 10–50×</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Production use</td><td>Yes — level 3 and 4 are designed for this</td><td>Rarely — timing changes can hide or alter the bug</td></tr>
    <tr><td>What you observe</td><td>What the developer chose to expose</td><td>Anything — but requires knowing where to look</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Long-running bugs</td><td>Excellent — output accumulates in logs</td><td>Poor — session-based, not persistent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Right when</td><td>Production deployments, Pis, containers, tight timing</td><td>Local development, memory bugs, unknown crash location</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>They complement each other.</strong> Use internal instrumentation (levels 3–4) to narrow the subsystem, then attach gdb or add a commented print (level 5) to get the exact value. The hierarchy is not "instead of gdb" — it's "everything you can do before gdb."
</div>

