<div class="quiz-block">

  <div class="question" data-question="1">
    <p>Q1. threadpool.c contains <code>#undef DEBUG</code> at the top before the <code>#ifdef DEBUG</code> block. What does this accomplish beyond just "not defining DEBUG"?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="#undef doesn't make a symbol undefined in the linker sense — it only affects preprocessor symbols. The linker doesn't know about DEBUG.">It prevents the linker from finding a DEBUG symbol in other object files</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. If someone compiles with -DDEBUG in CFLAGS (a common practice for debug builds), that would normally enable the DEBUG block in threadpool.c. The #undef cancels it specifically for this file, making the author's intent explicit: this debug output is never wanted in production, regardless of build flags.">It cancels any <code>-DDEBUG</code> compiler flag, making the off-state explicit even in debug builds</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="#undef has no runtime cost — it's a preprocessor directive that disappears before compilation even begins.">It reduces runtime overhead by clearing the DEBUG variable at startup</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="All #ifdef guards are removed at compile time. #undef affects only whether the block compiles; it doesn't change link behavior.">It tells the linker to strip all DEBUG-related symbols at link time</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="2">
    <p>Q2. In the FIFO-drop case in rtlsdrCallback(), the antiSpam counter is decremented (<code>--antiSpam</code>) rather than compared against a timestamp. When would you choose count-based over time-based antiSpam?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="mstime() is already used elsewhere in the same callback. Both forms are available.">When mstime() is not available in that code path</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both forms use a static variable. The storage cost is identical (one int64_t vs one int).">When memory is extremely constrained and you can't store an int64_t</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Count-based antiSpam ties the print rate to the call rate, which is stable at ~35 callbacks/sec for rtlsdr. '300 callbacks' is directly readable as 'roughly 8 seconds at 35/s.' Time-based requires checking the wall clock (a syscall) on every call. In a tight callback, avoiding even one syscall per callback matters.">When the call rate is stable and known, making N-count directly meaningful — and when avoiding the mstime() call per iteration matters</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both patterns are thread-safe as long as the static variable is only written by one thread. Neither requires a mutex for single-writer use.">When the function is called from multiple threads simultaneously</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="3">
    <p>Q3. A commented-out fprintf inside a per-sample conversion loop (like convert.c:52) would print 65,536 lines during a single lookup table initialization. Why keep the comment rather than deleting it?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The C compiler sees commented-out code as whitespace — it has zero impact on compilation time or binary size.">It reduces compilation time since the compiler skips commented code</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. The comment serves as a navigation aid for the next developer who needs to understand or debug the lookup table. It says: 'I debugged this exact loop; here's the print that revealed the values.' Without it, the next developer has to rediscover the same instrumentation point from scratch.">It's a breadcrumb: it marks the exact instrumentation point a previous developer needed, saving the next developer from rediscovering it</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Commented code is not dead code elimination in the compiler sense — it's simply text. The compiler ignores comments entirely.">It enables dead code elimination, which shrinks the binary</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Nothing in C prevents uncommenting code. The comment is a deliberate choice, not a compiler restriction.">It prevents future developers from accidentally enabling it</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question="4">
    <p>Q4. You need to investigate whether a network client is connecting correctly. You don't want to recompile, and the process is already running. Which level do you reach for?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Level 5 (commented-out fprintf) requires a recompile and restart. You said you don't want to recompile.">Level 5 — uncomment the fprintf near the connection code and recompile</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Level 1 (#ifdef CRCDEBUG) is a compile-time flag and won't help with network connection investigation.">Level 1 — compile with -DCRCDEBUG and restart</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Level 4 (--debug n, which sets Modes.debug_net) is enabled at startup with a command-line flag. You restart with --debug n and the networking subsystem becomes verbose with no recompile needed. This is exactly what debug flags are for.">Level 4 — restart with <code>--debug n</code> to enable Modes.debug_net</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Level 6 (crash-on-demand) would kill the process. It tests monitoring infrastructure, not networking logic.">Level 6 — use --devel provokeSegfault to reset the connection state</li>
    </ul>
