  <div class="question" data-question="5">
    <p>Q5. Level 4 debug flags can still be combined with antiSpam (level 3), as seen in rtlsdrCallback(). Why is this combination common?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="antiSpam has no effect on whether a flag is enabled — it only controls print frequency when the code is reached.">Because antiSpam acts as an additional flag that must be set alongside the debug flag</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. When you enable --debug K in a hot callback that fires 35x/second, the debug prints themselves would spam at 35 lines/sec. The antiSpam wraps the print so you still get the info (at a readable rate) rather than a wall of identical lines.">A debug flag in a hot callback still fires too fast without rate-limiting; antiSpam makes the output readable</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Performance cost of a debug flag check is a single branch — the antiSpam doesn't reduce that cost further.">To reduce the branch prediction penalty of the debug flag check</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="antiSpam doesn't add any thread safety. Thread-safety for shared state requires a mutex, not rate-limiting.">To make the debug output thread-safe in the multi-threaded reader</li>
    </ul>
