<h2>Check Your Understanding — Scenarios</h2>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Scenario A:</strong> You want to print the contents of every mag_buf as it passes through the decoder — both samples and metadata. Which level is appropriate and why? What's the risk if you choose the wrong level?
<br><br>
<a href="#" id="toggleA" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansA" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
<strong>Level 5 (commented-out fprintf)</strong>, not level 4. A mag_buf has up to 65,000 samples; printing every sample would produce ~2.3 million lines per second (35 buffers/sec × 65,536 samples). Even printing just the metadata (8 fields per buffer) produces 280 lines/sec — manageable but only for a few seconds of investigation, not production.<br><br>
<strong>Right approach:</strong> Add a commented fprintf that prints only the metadata fields (length, sampleTimestamp, mean_level, dropped) and gate it with antiSpam so it fires at most once per second. Mark it clearly for removal when done.<br><br>
<strong>Wrong level risk:</strong> A level 4 debug flag that prints per-sample data without antiSpam would make the decoder thread unable to keep up (it's doing 65k fprintf syscalls per callback), causing the very FIFO drops you're trying to investigate.
</div>
</div>

<div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
<strong>Scenario B:</strong> Your deployment uses a watchdog that should restart readsb if it dies. You want to verify the watchdog works before deploying to 200 sites. What do you use, and what do you verify?
<br><br>
<a href="#" id="toggleB" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansB" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
<strong>Level 6: <code>--devel provokeSegfault</code></strong>. Run readsb normally on one test node with this flag — it will initialize fully (666ms sleep), log the intent to stderr, then SIGSEGV. Verify:<br><br>
1. The watchdog/systemd detects the non-zero exit and restarts within expected time.<br>
2. A core dump is written (if configured) — check with <code>ls -lh /var/crash/</code> or the configured coredump path.<br>
3. The monitoring system alerts on the unexpected restart.<br>
4. The restarted instance initializes correctly without manual intervention.<br><br>
If any of these fail on the test node, you've found a gap in your infrastructure before deploying to 200 sites. That's exactly what level 6 is for.
</div>
</div>

